{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"set -euo pipefail\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\np=Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/metaphysics/sources/artifacts/source-02-grounding-is-not-a-strict-order--7c76f525b4e3/source.md')\ntext=p.read_text(errors='replace')\nfor a,b in [('\\\\u00a0',' '),('\\\\u00ad',''),('‐','-'),('‑','-'),('–','-'),('—','-')]: text=text.replace(a,b)\ntext=re.sub(r'[ \\\\t\\\\r\\\\f\\\\v]+',' ',text)\ntext=re.sub(r'\\\\n\\\\s*\\\\n+','\\\\n\\\\n',text)\nparas=[]\nfor block in text.split('\\\\n\\\\n'):\n    block=re.sub(r'\\\\s*\\\\n\\\\s*',' ',block).strip()\n    if block: paras.append(block)\nfor start,end,label in [(5,25,'intro'),(51,68,'grounding/truthmaking'),(164,235,'schaffer'),(277,331,'transitivity'),(339,397,'irreflexivity'),(514,595,'asymmetry'),(691,724,'contrastive/conclusion')]:\n    print(f'\\\\n===== {label} paras {start}-{end} =====')\n    for i in range(start,end):\n        if i < len(paras):\n            print(f'[{i}] {paras[i]}\\\\n')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"set -euo pipefail\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\np=Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/metaphysics/sources/artifacts/source-02-grounding-is-not-a-strict-order--7c76f525b4e3/source.md')\ntext=p.read_text(errors='replace')\nfor a,b in [('\\\\u00a0',' '),('\\\\u00ad',''),('‐','-'),('‑','-'),('–','-'),('—','-')]: text=text.replace(a,b)\ntext=re.sub(r'[ \\\\t\\\\r\\\\f\\\\v]+',' ',text)\ntext=re.sub(r'\\\\n\\\\s*\\\\n+','\\\\n\\\\n',text)\nparas=[]\nfor block in text.split('\\\\n\\\\n'):\n    block=re.sub(r'\\\\s*\\\\n\\\\s*',' ',block).strip()\n    if block: paras.append(block)\nfor start,end,label in [(5,25,'intro'),(51,68,'grounding/truthmaking'),(164,235,'schaffer'),(277,331,'transitivity'),(339,397,'irreflexivity'),(514,595,'asymmetry'),(691,724,'contrastive/conclusion')]:\n    print(f'\\\\n===== {label} paras {start}-{end} =====')\n    for i in range(start,end):\n        if i < len(paras):\n            print(f'[{i}] {paras[i]}\\\\n')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n===== intro paras 5-25 =====\n[5] 1. A strict order is a relation that is transitive, irreflexive, and asymmetric. My\n\n[6] aim in this paper is to argue that grounding is not a strict order. More precisely, I\n\n[7] shall argue that grounding is neither transitive, nor irreflexive, nor asymmetric.2\n\n[8] This is controversial and original. It is controversial because some philosophers\n\n[9] maintain that grounding is a strict order, or that it has some of the three features\n\n[10] necessary for being a strict order (for instance, Correia (2010) and Raven (2013:\n\n[11] 193-94) think it has all three; Fine thinks it is irreflexive and transitive (2010:\n\n[12] 100), which entails it is also asymmetric (see also Fine 2012: 56); Audi, Bennett,\n\n[13] Rosen, and Schaffer think that it is irreflexive and asymmetric (Audi 2012a: 102,\n\n[14] Audi 2012b: 691-92, Bennett forthcoming, Schaffer 2009: 364, Rosen 2010:\n\n[15] 115-116)). And it is original because although some philosophers have\n\n[16] maintained that grounding is not a strict order because of lacking one of the\n\n[17] three features, as far as I know no one has argued that it lacks all three of them\n\n[18] (Schaffer (2012) has argued that grounding is not transitive, Thompson (2014)\n\n[19] has argued that it is not asymmetric, and Correia (2014) has rejected its\n\n[20] irreflexivity).3 Furthermore the way in which I shall argue for my thesis is\n\n[21] original, since some of my examples that grounding is neither irreflexive, nor\n\n[22] asymmetric, nor transitive will be examples of truthmaking where irreflexivity,\n\n[23] asymmetry, and transitivity fail.\n\n[24] The structure of the paper is as follows. In Section 2 I shall characterize\n\n\n===== grounding/truthmaking paras 51-68 =====\n[51] 2. Truthmaking is the relation that obtains between a true proposition and that\n\n[52] in virtue of which it is true, its truthmaker. For instance, according to many, the\n\n[53] fact that Socrates is white is the truthmaker of the proposition <Socrates is\n\n[54] white>.4 So the fact that Socrates is white and the proposition <Socrates is\n\n[55] white> are related by the truthmaking relation. Similarly, according to many,\n\n[56] Socrates is the truthmaker of the proposition <Socrates exists>. So Socrates and\n\n[57] the proposition <Socrates exists> are related by the truthmaking relation.\n\n[58] Is truthmaking a case of grounding? I think it is, and others think so as\n\n[59] well (Schaffer 2009: 375, Liggins 2012: 269; cf. Schnieder 2006: 30). The relation\n\n[60] between truthmaker and truth seems analogous to the relation between member\n\n[61] and set, between part and whole, and between being a cruel action and being a\n\n[62] wrong action, and these are all initially plausible cases of ground and grounded.\n\n[63] It is thus initially plausible that truthmaking is a case of grounding.\n\n[64] Sometimes grounding is characterized as a non--causal relation of\n\n[65] dependence (Audi 2012a: 101, 2012b: 690). But then truthmaking is not a case\n\n[66] of grounding, since the proposition <Socrates is white> does not depend on the\n\n[67] fact that Socrates is white. What depends on the fact that Socrates is white is not\n\n\n===== schaffer paras 164-235 =====\n[164] 3. Schaffer has presented the following counterexample to transitivity. Consider\n\n[165] the set S = {a, b, c}. Schaffer argues that (a) the fact that c is a member of S\n\n[166] grounds the fact that S has exactly three members and (b) the fact that S has\n\n[167] exactly three members grounds the fact that S has finitely many members, but\n\n[168] (c) it is not the case that the fact that c is a member of S grounds the fact that S\n\n[169] has finitely many members (Schaffer 2012: 127-128).\n\n[170] Schaffer supports (a) by saying that if c were not a member of S then S\n\n[171] would have had two members and not three. Given that sets have their members\n\n[172] necessarily, it is controversial that if S had not had c as a member, it would have\n\n[173] 6\n\n[174] had two members. But it is still plausible that if it had not had c as a member, it\n\n[175] would not have had three members, since it would not have existed. Still,\n\n[176] Schaffer’s reasoning treats the fact that something is counterfactually necessary\n\n[177] for another fact as a sufficient condition of the former fact being a ground of the\n\n[178] latter, where a fact [P] is counterfactually necessary for a fact [Q] if and only if\n\n[179] had [P] not obtained, [Q] would not have obtained.\n\n[180] This differs from Schaffer’s support for (c). For he says that (c) is true\n\n[181] because S would have been finite even without c as a member. But, it might be\n\n[182] objected, S would not have been finite without c as a member, since S would not\n\n[183] have existed. This problem can be circumvented by changing the example and\n\n[184] using a property that contingently has three instances (as noted by Litland 2013:\n\n[185] 25-26). So I shall ignore this problem and continue using Schaffer’s example of\n\n[186] set S.\n\n[187] So, according to Schaffer, the fact that c is a member of S does not ground\n\n[188] the fact that S has finitely many members because if c had not been a member of\n\n[189] S, S would still have been finite. But this means that a fact being counterfactually\n\n[190] necessary for another fact is a necessary condition of the former grounding the\n\n[191] latter. And given what he says about (a), that a fact is counterfactually necessary\n\n[192] for another fact is necessary and sufficient for the former grounding the latter.\n\n[193] But that a fact is counterfactually necessary for another fact is a necessary\n\n[194] condition for the former grounding the latter conflicts with (b), since the fact\n\n[195] that S has exactly three members is not counterfactually necessary for the fact\n\n[196] that S has finitely many members. But all Schaffer says about (b) is that it is\n\n[197] plausible because having finitely many members is a determinable, of which\n\n[198] having exactly three members is a determinate (2012: 127). So perhaps the idea\n\n[199] is that, provided property F is not a determinate of property G, if the fact that\n\n[200] something is F grounds the fact that it is G, that it is F must be counterfactually\n\n[201] necessary for its being G. But it is not clear what the rationale for exempting the\n\n[202] case of determinates and determinables would be. Furthermore, it seems that\n\n[203] other exceptions will be needed since it is a standard idea that disjunctive facts\n\n[204] are grounded in each of their true disjuncts, despite the latter often not being\n\n[205] counterfactually necessary for the former. But then what is difficult to see is why\n\n[206] in the case of the facts that c is a member of S and that S has finitely many\n\n[207] 7\n\n[208] members, for the former to ground the latter, the former must be\n\n[209] counterfactually necessary for the latter.\n\n[210] Now, in private correspondence Schaffer has said that his intention was to\n\n[211] use the counterfactual test not to establish his claims (a) and (c) but as a\n\n[212] heuristic to buttress the intuitions that (a) and (c) are correct. But since no\n\n[213] reason has been given for why the counterfactual test is a good heuristic in the\n\n[214] cases in which it is supposed to be a good heuristic, it is not clear that it\n\n[215] buttresses any intuitions. Furthermore, this methodology seems impotent to\n\n[216] convince anyone who does not share the initial intuitions. This is my own case.\n\n[217] Indeed, I reject (a). For the identity of its members has nothing to do with the\n\n[218] fact that S has exactly three of them - what grounds the fact that S has exactly\n\n[219] three members is the fact that there is something, something else, and a third\n\n[220] thing such that none of them is identical with either of the others, all three are\n\n[221] members of S, and nothing else is.5\n\n[222] Schaffer produces two other counterexamples to the transitivity of\n\n[223] grounding. One is the dented sphere case. But this case has exactly the same\n\n[224] structure as the case of the three--membered set S, and so what I said about the\n\n[225] latter applies to the former. The other case is that of Cadmus’ meow. According\n\n[226] to Schaffer, (d) the fact that the creature was produced from the meeting of this\n\n[227] sperm and that ovum grounds the fact that Cadmus is meowing and (e) the fact\n\n[228] that Cadmus is meowing grounds the fact that something is meowing, but (f) the\n\n[229] fact that the creature was produced from the meeting of this sperm and that\n\n[230] ovum does not ground the fact that something is meowing. Given what Schaffer\n\n[231] says about (d), (e), and (f), it is not clear that this case is intended to have the\n\n[232] same structure as the other two.\n\n[233] I think this example is not a failure of transitivity. In defense of (d)\n\n[234] Schaffer says that, given origin essentialism, (d) is plausible, since being\n\n\n===== transitivity paras 277-331 =====\n[277] 4. In this section I shall argue that truthmaking is not transitive. Consider (1) and\n\n[278] (2):\n\n[279] (1) The fact that Socrates is white makes <Socrates is white> true.\n\n[280] (2) <Socrates is white> makes <There are propositions> true.\n\n[281] (1) is plausible for truthmaker theorists. And so is (2). For it is plausible that the\n\n[282] truthmakers for an existential generalization are its instances if those instances\n\n[283] are necessarily its instances. But it is also plausible that propositions are\n\n[284] necessarily propositions. Thus any proposition is a truthmaker for <There are\n\n[285] propositions>.\n\n[286] Now, transitivity would give us (3):\n\n[287] (3) The fact that Socrates is white makes <There are propositions> true.\n\n[288] But (3) is false. The proposition <There are propositions> is not true in virtue of\n\n[289] the fact that Socrates is white. Although the fact that Socrates is white makes\n\n[290] true a truthmaker of <There are propositions>, the fact that Socrates is white is\n\n[291] not that in virtue of which <There are propositions> is true. Indeed, the fact that\n\n[292] Socrates is white is not even that in virtue of which <Socrates is white> exists,\n\n[293] and so one should not expect it to be that in virtue of which <There are\n\n[294] propositions> is true. Only that in virtue of which propositions exist can be the\n\n[295] truthmaker of <There are propositions>. Thus (3) is false, and transitivity fails.6\n\n[296] Raven says that truthmaking cannot be a species of grounding because\n\n[297] the relata of truthmaking are of different types and this precludes chaining\n\n[298] (Raven 2013: 194; cf. Fine 2012: 44-45). But not all the relata of a truthmaking\n\n[299] relation are of different types, as this example illustrates. A proposition can both\n\n[300] be made true and make true another proposition. This is the case with <Socrates\n\n[301] is white> in the present example.\n\n[302] 6 After writing and submitting this paper I learnt, through a referee, of Aaron Griffith’s paper,\n\n[303] where he presents a counterexample to the transitivity of truthmaking very similar to mine\n\n[304] (Griffith 2014: 205). He also denies the irreflexivity and asymmetry of truthmaking, though his\n\n[305] examples for this claim are different from mine. More importantly, he seems to think that\n\n[306] grounding is irreflexive, asymmetric and transitive (2014: 205).\n\n[307] 10\n\n[308] Is the alethic--fact relation non--transitive? I am not in a position to argue\n\n[309] for such a claim. Consider, for instance, the following three claims:\n\n[310] (4) The fact that Socrates is white grounds the fact that <Socrates is white> is\n\n[311] true.\n\n[312] (5) The fact that <Socrates is white> is true grounds the fact that <There are\n\n[313] propositions> is true.\n\n[314] (6) It is not the case that the fact that Socrates is white grounds the fact that\n\n[315] <There are propositions> is true.\n\n[316] These three claims do not show that alethic--fact grounding is non--transitive, for\n\n[317] although (4) and (6) are true, (5) is false. What <There are propositions> is true\n\n[318] in virtue of is not the fact that <Socrates is white> is true, but rather the fact that\n\n[319] <Socrates is white> exists.\n\n[320] One might then think that the following three claims show that alethic-- fact grounding is non--transitive:\n\n[321] (4) The fact that Socrates is white grounds the fact that <Socrates is white> is\n\n[322] true.\n\n[323] (7) The fact that <Socrates is white> is true grounds the fact that <There are true\n\n[324] propositions> is true.\n\n[325] (8) It is not the case that the fact that Socrates is white grounds the fact that\n\n[326] <There are true propositions> is true.\n\n[327] But these three claims do not show that alethic--fact grounding is non--transitive.\n\n[328] For although both (4) and (7) are true, I see no reason to accept (8) that does not\n\n[329] assume that alethic--fact grounding is non--transitive.7\n\n[330] 7 Points made by Nick Jones and Ezequiel Zerbudis made me see that I am not in a position to\n\n\n===== irreflexivity paras 339-397 =====\n[339] 5. In this section I shall argue that grounding is not irreflexive. Consider the\n\n[340] proposition A: <A exists>. This proposition says of itself that it exists. Let us\n\n[341] suppose that A exists. If so, A is true. What makes it true? According to standard\n\n[342] truthmaking ideas, a proposition of the form <e exists> is made true by e itself.\n\n[343] Thus, if it exists, A is made true by itself. Thus truthmaking is not irreflexive, and\n\n[344] therefore neither is grounding.\n\n[345] But there is another reason why grounding is not irreflexive, since alethic-- fact grounding also fails irreflexivity. To see this, consider the truth--teller, B: <B\n\n[346] is true>. This proposition says of itself that it is true. Let us suppose that B is\n\n[347] indeed true. What grounds the fact that it is true? The fact stated by B itself. But\n\n[348] what B states is that it itself is true. Thus, if B is true, the fact that B is true\n\n[349] grounds the fact that B is true. Therefore, alethic--fact grounding is not irreflexive.\n\n[350] (4), (7), and (8). Tuomas Tahko (2013) argues that what he calls truth--grounding is not\n\n[351] transitive, and what he calls truth--grounding seems to be what I am calling here alethic--fact\n\n[352] grounding. Simplifying a little bit, he argues that while the fact that bottle b exists is grounded in\n\n[353] the fact that b has a stable macrophysical structure, and the fact that b has a stable macrophysical\n\n[354] structure is grounded in the fact that the Pauli Exclusion Principle holds, it is not the case that the\n\n[355] fact that b exists is grounded in the fact that the Pauli Exclusion principle holds (2013: 336-337).\n\n[356] The problem with this is that it does not seem to counterexemplify the transitivity of truth-- grounding in particular, since no fact involved in this case is a fact about something’s being true.\n\n[357] But I do not think the example even works against the transitivity of grounding in general. Tahko\n\n[358] thinks that general laws like the Pauli Exclusion Principle in no way contribute towards\n\n[359] answering the question about the existence of bottle b (2013: 337). But if the fact that the Pauli\n\n[360] Exclusion principle holds grounds the fact that b has a stable macrophysical structure, and this\n\n[361] latter fact grounds the fact that b exists, then I see no reason, other than assuming that\n\n[362] transitivity must fail, why the fact that the Pauli Exclusion Principle holds should not ground the\n\n[363] fact that bottle b exists or even the fact that <bottle b exists> is true. One might try reformulating\n\n[364] the example as follows: the fact that <b has a stable macrophysical structure> is true grounds the\n\n[365] fact that <b exists> is true, the fact that the Pauli Exclusion Principle holds grounds the fact that\n\n[366] <b has a stable macrophysical structure> is true, but it is not the case that the fact that the Pauli\n\n[367] Exclusion Principle holds grounds the fact that <b exists> is true. But this reformulation has the\n\n[368] additional problem that the first claim, that the fact that <b has a stable macrophysical structure>\n\n[369] is true grounds the fact that <b exists> is true, seems incorrect: what grounds the truth of <b\n\n[370] exists> is, in any case, something about b, say that it has a stable macrophysical structure, but not\n\n[371] something about the truth of another proposition.\n\n[372] 12\n\n[373] And since alethic--fact grounding is a species of grounding, grounding is not\n\n[374] irreflexive.8 (Note that if A is its own truthmaker, it is its only truthmaker, and if\n\n[375] the fact that B is true is grounded by the fact that B is true, the fact that B is true\n\n[376] is its sole ground. This means that A is its own full ground and the fact that B is\n\n[377] true is its own full ground).9\n\n[378] Kripke has proposed a theory of truth in which the concept of\n\n[379] groundedness plays a central role. The truthbearers in Kripke’s theory are\n\n[380] sentences. On Kripke’s theory, the truth--teller, the sentence that says of itself\n\n[381] that it is true, is ungrounded (Kripke: 2011: 85, 91). Thus what I am proposing\n\n[382] here seems to conflict with Kripke’s theory of truth.\n\n[383] But it is important to appreciate the real point of conflict. On Kripke’s\n\n[384] theory that a sentence is ungrounded basically means that its truth--value is not\n\n[385] ultimately determined by non--semantic facts (Kripke 2011: 88). The motivation\n\n[386] of Kripke’s theory is to give an explanation of truth that avoids the semantic\n\n[387] paradoxes, and he does this in a way that accounts for how someone who\n\n[388] initially does not understand the truth predicate, but who understands other\n\n[389] non--semantic predicates, would learn to use the truth predicate (2011: 85). At\n\n[390] the base of Kripke’s construction there are sentences featuring only non-- semantic predicates. The assignment of truth--values to sentences featuring non-- semantic predicates determines a minimal fixed point in which the truth value of\n\n[391] sentences featuring semantic predicates is ultimately determined by the truth\n\n[392] value of sentences featuring only non--semantic predicates. The sentences that do\n\n[393] not have a truth--value in the minimal fixed points are ungrounded. Thus for\n\n[394] Kripke ‘ungrounded sentence’ means ‘sentence not ultimately grounded in non-- semantic facts’.\n\n[395] But, as Kripke is of course aware, the truth--teller is not a paradoxical\n\n[396] sentence. The truth--teller can consistently be assigned the truth--value true, and\n\n\n===== asymmetry paras 514-595 =====\n[514] 6. If truthmaking and alethic--fact grounding are asymmetric, they must be\n\n[515] irreflexive. Their non--irreflexivity, however, guarantees their non--asymmetry\n\n[516] only by guaranteeing that cases of symmetric truthmaking and alethic--fact\n\n[517] grounding are cases of reflexive truthmaking and alethic--fact grounding. But if\n\n[518] what I have said above is accepted, it will not be difficult to see that truthmaking\n\n[519] and grounding are non--asymmetric in the full sense of there being distinct\n\n[520] entities that ground each other.\n\n[521] Consider the propositions C = <D exists> and D = <C exists>, and assume\n\n[522] that both of them exist. The truthmaker of C is D, and the truthmaker of D is C.\n\n[523] Therefore truthmaking is not asymmetric, and so neither is grounding.\n\n[524] Now consider the propositions E = <F is true> and F = <E is true>, and\n\n[525] assume they are both true. If so, given what they say, the fact that E is true\n\n[526] grounds the fact that F is true, and the fact that F is true grounds the fact that E is\n\n[527] true.10 Therefore, alethic--fact grounding is not asymmetric, and so neither is\n\n[528] grounding.\n\n[529] But there is a natural objection to this. This is that grounding must be\n\n[530] asymmetric because the ground is more fundamental than the grounded, and\n\n[531] being more fundamental than is an asymmetric relation (cf. Rosen 2010: 116,\n\n[532] Barnes 2014, Bennett forthcoming). The same kind of consideration can be used\n\n[533] to argue that grounding must be irreflexive, since being more fundamental than is\n\n[534] an irreflexive relation.\n\n[535] 10 Thompson (2014) has given a similar example.\n\n[536] 17\n\n[537] Now, the relations of grounding and being more fundamental than are not\n\n[538] the same relation. For something might be more fundamental than another thing\n\n[539] without grounding it. This will be the case, for instance, if the former thing is\n\n[540] fundamental and the latter one is grounded, but grounded in something other\n\n[541] than the former thing. But the point that grounding must be irreflexive and\n\n[542] asymmetric need not rely on an identification of the relations of grounding and\n\n[543] being more fundamental than but only on the fact that when x grounds y, x must\n\n[544] be more fundamental than y. But I shall argue that either the fact that x grounds y\n\n[545] does not entail that x is more fundamental than y or there is no reason to think\n\n[546] that it does.\n\n[547] Let us assume that the more fundamental than relation is asymmetric and\n\n[548] irreflexive. But what is it to be more fundamental than something else? One\n\n[549] answer is that it is to be closer to a fundamental (that is, ungrounded) entity than\n\n[550] another thing (where it is understood that a fundamental entity is as close to a\n\n[551] fundamental entity - itself - as anything can be). And the obvious way to\n\n[552] understand this idea is that x is closer to a fundamental entity than y if and only\n\n[553] if, in their chain of grounding, there are fewer elements between x and a\n\n[554] fundamental entity than between y and a fundamental entity.\n\n[555] Now the relation of grounding is not restricted to finite chains. Nothing in\n\n[556] the idea of grounding excludes the possibility of chains of grounding containing\n\n[557] infinitely many distinct elements and such that there is no first element, no\n\n[558] ungrounded ground. For instance, if wholes are grounded in their parts, a gunky\n\n[559] whole, i.e. a whole all of whose parts have proper parts, would be at the top of an\n\n[560] infinitely long chain of grounding with no first element. Now take two members\n\n[561] of such a chain, x and y, such that x grounds y. Is x more fundamental than y? No,\n\n[562] for there is no fundamental entity in their chain of grounding, and therefore it is\n\n[563] not the case that there are fewer elements between x and a fundamental entity\n\n[564] than between y and a fundamental entity.\n\n[565] If so, that x grounds y does not entail that x is more fundamental than y,\n\n[566] and so the fact that reflexive and symmetric cases of grounding are cases in\n\n[567] which the more fundamental than relation does not obtain between the ground\n\n[568] and the grounded is no objection to them. Reflexive and symmetric cases of\n\n[569] grounding are like bottomless chains of grounding: cases of grounding without\n\n[570] 18\n\n[571] different degrees of fundamentality. And in all three cases the reason why there\n\n[572] are no different degrees of fundamentality is that there are no fundamental\n\n[573] entities in the chains of grounding: neither bottomless chains of grounding, nor\n\n[574] ‘reflexive’ chains of grounding, nor ‘symmetric’ chains of grounding, contain\n\n[575] fundamental, ungrounded entities.11\n\n[576] It might be objected that there cannot be bottomless grounding chains,\n\n[577] that is, that every chain must end in an ungrounded ground. I do not see why\n\n[578] there cannot be such ‘bottomless’ chains. But I do not need their possibility to\n\n[579] make my point. For it is sufficient that there can be dense chains of grounding,\n\n[580] that is, chains of grounding such that between any two elements there is a\n\n[581] further element. In this case it will also be true that no element of such a chain\n\n[582] will be closer to a fundamental entity than any other element of it (except, of\n\n[583] course, when the member in question is the ungrounded ground of the chain).\n\n[584] It might be objected that the real number line is dense but one can make\n\n[585] sense of one positive real being closer to zero than another positive real. But this\n\n[586] is because we can independently make sense of the notion of one positive real\n\n[587] being bigger or smaller than another one. In this case we are working with a\n\n[588] notion of relative fundamentality according to which x is closer to a fundamental\n\n[589] entity than y if and only if, in their respective chains of grounding, there are\n\n[590] fewer elements between x and a fundamental entity than between y and a\n\n[591] fundamental entity.\n\n[592] A better objection would be against the characterization I have given of\n\n[593] what it is to be more fundamental than something else. Perhaps x is more\n\n[594] 11 Note that there is a sense in which a self--grounder is more fundamental than something\n\n\n===== contrastive/conclusion paras 691-724 =====\n[691] 7. Schaffer has proposed a contrastive account of grounding according to which\n\n[692] grounding is a quaternary relation of the form: the fact that F rather than the fact\n\n[693] that F* grounds the fact that G rather than the fact that G*, where the facts that F\n\n[694] and G are required to be obtaining facts and the facts that F* and G* are required\n\n[695] to be non--obtaining facts. Schaffer thinks that grounding respects the principles\n\n[696] of transitivity, asymmetry, and reflexivity when these are formulated for\n\n[697] differences. Thus:\n\n[698] Differential irreflexivity: It is not the case that the fact that F rather than F*\n\n[699] grounds the fact that F rather than F*.\n\n[700] Differential asymmetry: If the fact that F rather than F* grounds the fact that G\n\n[701] rather than G*, then it is not the case that the fact that G rather than G* grounds\n\n[702] the fact that F rather than F*\n\n[703] Differential transitivity: If the fact that F rather than F* grounds the fact that G\n\n[704] rather than G*, and the fact that G rather than G* grounds the fact that H rather\n\n[705] than H*, then the fact that F rather than F* grounds the fact that H rather than H*.\n\n[706] Schaffer argues that his counterexamples to the transitivity of grounding are not\n\n[707] counterexamples to the differential transitivity of grounding. And he suggests\n\n[708] (2012: 133, fn. 17) that Jenkins’ counterexample to the irreflexivity of grounding\n\n[709] does not threaten differential irreflexivity. This suggests that a quaternary\n\n[710] relation of grounding may be a strict order.\n\n[711] But my examples are also examples against differential irreflexivity and\n\n[712] differential asymmetry of grounding. The fact that B is true rather than B is not\n\n[713] true grounds the fact that B is true rather than B is not true. So differential\n\n[714] 22\n\n[715] irreflexivity fails. And the fact that E is true rather than E is not true grounds the\n\n[716] fact that F is true rather than F is not true, and the fact that F is true rather than F\n\n[717] is not true grounds the fact that E is true rather than E is not true. So differential\n\n[718] asymmetry fails.\n\n[719] Therefore, taking grounding to be a quaternary relation does not make it\n\n[720] a strict order.\n\n[721] 8. To conclude, truthmaking is neither transitive, nor irreflexive, nor asymmetric,\n\n[722] and alethic--fact grounding is neither irreflexive nor asymmetric. Since those two\n\n[723] relations are species or cases of grounding, grounding is neither transitive, nor\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"set -euo pipefail\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\np=Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/metaphysics/sources/artifacts/source-02-grounding-is-not-a-strict-order--7c76f525b4e3/source.md')\ntext=p.read_text(errors='replace')\nfor a,b in [('\\\\u00a0',' '),('\\\\u00ad',''),('‐','-'),('‑','-'),('–','-'),('—','-')]: text=text.replace(a,b)\ntext=re.sub(r'[ \\\\t\\\\r\\\\f\\\\v]+',' ',text)\ntext=re.sub(r'\\\\n\\\\s*\\\\n+','\\\\n\\\\n',text)\nparas=[]\nfor block in text.split('\\\\n\\\\n'):\n    block=re.sub(r'\\\\s*\\\\n\\\\s*',' ',block).strip()\n    if block: paras.append(block)\nfor start,end,label in [(187,235,'Schaffer analysis'),(277,331,'Truthmaking transitivity'),(339,397,'Irreflexivity'),(514,545,'Asymmetry examples'),(545,595,'Fundamentality objection'),(595,678,'More fundamentality continuation'),(721,724,'Conclusion')]:\n    print(f'\\\\n===== {label} =====')\n    for i in range(start,end):\n        if i < len(paras): print(f'[{i}] {paras[i]}\\\\n')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"set -euo pipefail\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\np=Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/metaphysics/sources/artifacts/source-02-grounding-is-not-a-strict-order--7c76f525b4e3/source.md')\ntext=p.read_text(errors='replace')\nfor a,b in [('\\\\u00a0',' '),('\\\\u00ad',''),('‐','-'),('‑','-'),('–','-'),('—','-')]: text=text.replace(a,b)\ntext=re.sub(r'[ \\\\t\\\\r\\\\f\\\\v]+',' ',text)\ntext=re.sub(r'\\\\n\\\\s*\\\\n+','\\\\n\\\\n',text)\nparas=[]\nfor block in text.split('\\\\n\\\\n'):\n    block=re.sub(r'\\\\s*\\\\n\\\\s*',' ',block).strip()\n    if block: paras.append(block)\nfor start,end,label in [(187,235,'Schaffer analysis'),(277,331,'Truthmaking transitivity'),(339,397,'Irreflexivity'),(514,545,'Asymmetry examples'),(545,595,'Fundamentality objection'),(595,678,'More fundamentality continuation'),(721,724,'Conclusion')]:\n    print(f'\\\\n===== {label} =====')\n    for i in range(start,end):\n        if i < len(paras): print(f'[{i}] {paras[i]}\\\\n')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n===== Schaffer analysis =====\n[187] So, according to Schaffer, the fact that c is a member of S does not ground\n\n[188] the fact that S has finitely many members because if c had not been a member of\n\n[189] S, S would still have been finite. But this means that a fact being counterfactually\n\n[190] necessary for another fact is a necessary condition of the former grounding the\n\n[191] latter. And given what he says about (a), that a fact is counterfactually necessary\n\n[192] for another fact is necessary and sufficient for the former grounding the latter.\n\n[193] But that a fact is counterfactually necessary for another fact is a necessary\n\n[194] condition for the former grounding the latter conflicts with (b), since the fact\n\n[195] that S has exactly three members is not counterfactually necessary for the fact\n\n[196] that S has finitely many members. But all Schaffer says about (b) is that it is\n\n[197] plausible because having finitely many members is a determinable, of which\n\n[198] having exactly three members is a determinate (2012: 127). So perhaps the idea\n\n[199] is that, provided property F is not a determinate of property G, if the fact that\n\n[200] something is F grounds the fact that it is G, that it is F must be counterfactually\n\n[201] necessary for its being G. But it is not clear what the rationale for exempting the\n\n[202] case of determinates and determinables would be. Furthermore, it seems that\n\n[203] other exceptions will be needed since it is a standard idea that disjunctive facts\n\n[204] are grounded in each of their true disjuncts, despite the latter often not being\n\n[205] counterfactually necessary for the former. But then what is difficult to see is why\n\n[206] in the case of the facts that c is a member of S and that S has finitely many\n\n[207] 7\n\n[208] members, for the former to ground the latter, the former must be\n\n[209] counterfactually necessary for the latter.\n\n[210] Now, in private correspondence Schaffer has said that his intention was to\n\n[211] use the counterfactual test not to establish his claims (a) and (c) but as a\n\n[212] heuristic to buttress the intuitions that (a) and (c) are correct. But since no\n\n[213] reason has been given for why the counterfactual test is a good heuristic in the\n\n[214] cases in which it is supposed to be a good heuristic, it is not clear that it\n\n[215] buttresses any intuitions. Furthermore, this methodology seems impotent to\n\n[216] convince anyone who does not share the initial intuitions. This is my own case.\n\n[217] Indeed, I reject (a). For the identity of its members has nothing to do with the\n\n[218] fact that S has exactly three of them - what grounds the fact that S has exactly\n\n[219] three members is the fact that there is something, something else, and a third\n\n[220] thing such that none of them is identical with either of the others, all three are\n\n[221] members of S, and nothing else is.5\n\n[222] Schaffer produces two other counterexamples to the transitivity of\n\n[223] grounding. One is the dented sphere case. But this case has exactly the same\n\n[224] structure as the case of the three--membered set S, and so what I said about the\n\n[225] latter applies to the former. The other case is that of Cadmus’ meow. According\n\n[226] to Schaffer, (d) the fact that the creature was produced from the meeting of this\n\n[227] sperm and that ovum grounds the fact that Cadmus is meowing and (e) the fact\n\n[228] that Cadmus is meowing grounds the fact that something is meowing, but (f) the\n\n[229] fact that the creature was produced from the meeting of this sperm and that\n\n[230] ovum does not ground the fact that something is meowing. Given what Schaffer\n\n[231] says about (d), (e), and (f), it is not clear that this case is intended to have the\n\n[232] same structure as the other two.\n\n[233] I think this example is not a failure of transitivity. In defense of (d)\n\n[234] Schaffer says that, given origin essentialism, (d) is plausible, since being\n\n\n===== Truthmaking transitivity =====\n[277] 4. In this section I shall argue that truthmaking is not transitive. Consider (1) and\n\n[278] (2):\n\n[279] (1) The fact that Socrates is white makes <Socrates is white> true.\n\n[280] (2) <Socrates is white> makes <There are propositions> true.\n\n[281] (1) is plausible for truthmaker theorists. And so is (2). For it is plausible that the\n\n[282] truthmakers for an existential generalization are its instances if those instances\n\n[283] are necessarily its instances. But it is also plausible that propositions are\n\n[284] necessarily propositions. Thus any proposition is a truthmaker for <There are\n\n[285] propositions>.\n\n[286] Now, transitivity would give us (3):\n\n[287] (3) The fact that Socrates is white makes <There are propositions> true.\n\n[288] But (3) is false. The proposition <There are propositions> is not true in virtue of\n\n[289] the fact that Socrates is white. Although the fact that Socrates is white makes\n\n[290] true a truthmaker of <There are propositions>, the fact that Socrates is white is\n\n[291] not that in virtue of which <There are propositions> is true. Indeed, the fact that\n\n[292] Socrates is white is not even that in virtue of which <Socrates is white> exists,\n\n[293] and so one should not expect it to be that in virtue of which <There are\n\n[294] propositions> is true. Only that in virtue of which propositions exist can be the\n\n[295] truthmaker of <There are propositions>. Thus (3) is false, and transitivity fails.6\n\n[296] Raven says that truthmaking cannot be a species of grounding because\n\n[297] the relata of truthmaking are of different types and this precludes chaining\n\n[298] (Raven 2013: 194; cf. Fine 2012: 44-45). But not all the relata of a truthmaking\n\n[299] relation are of different types, as this example illustrates. A proposition can both\n\n[300] be made true and make true another proposition. This is the case with <Socrates\n\n[301] is white> in the present example.\n\n[302] 6 After writing and submitting this paper I learnt, through a referee, of Aaron Griffith’s paper,\n\n[303] where he presents a counterexample to the transitivity of truthmaking very similar to mine\n\n[304] (Griffith 2014: 205). He also denies the irreflexivity and asymmetry of truthmaking, though his\n\n[305] examples for this claim are different from mine. More importantly, he seems to think that\n\n[306] grounding is irreflexive, asymmetric and transitive (2014: 205).\n\n[307] 10\n\n[308] Is the alethic--fact relation non--transitive? I am not in a position to argue\n\n[309] for such a claim. Consider, for instance, the following three claims:\n\n[310] (4) The fact that Socrates is white grounds the fact that <Socrates is white> is\n\n[311] true.\n\n[312] (5) The fact that <Socrates is white> is true grounds the fact that <There are\n\n[313] propositions> is true.\n\n[314] (6) It is not the case that the fact that Socrates is white grounds the fact that\n\n[315] <There are propositions> is true.\n\n[316] These three claims do not show that alethic--fact grounding is non--transitive, for\n\n[317] although (4) and (6) are true, (5) is false. What <There are propositions> is true\n\n[318] in virtue of is not the fact that <Socrates is white> is true, but rather the fact that\n\n[319] <Socrates is white> exists.\n\n[320] One might then think that the following three claims show that alethic-- fact grounding is non--transitive:\n\n[321] (4) The fact that Socrates is white grounds the fact that <Socrates is white> is\n\n[322] true.\n\n[323] (7) The fact that <Socrates is white> is true grounds the fact that <There are true\n\n[324] propositions> is true.\n\n[325] (8) It is not the case that the fact that Socrates is white grounds the fact that\n\n[326] <There are true propositions> is true.\n\n[327] But these three claims do not show that alethic--fact grounding is non--transitive.\n\n[328] For although both (4) and (7) are true, I see no reason to accept (8) that does not\n\n[329] assume that alethic--fact grounding is non--transitive.7\n\n[330] 7 Points made by Nick Jones and Ezequiel Zerbudis made me see that I am not in a position to\n\n\n===== Irreflexivity =====\n[339] 5. In this section I shall argue that grounding is not irreflexive. Consider the\n\n[340] proposition A: <A exists>. This proposition says of itself that it exists. Let us\n\n[341] suppose that A exists. If so, A is true. What makes it true? According to standard\n\n[342] truthmaking ideas, a proposition of the form <e exists> is made true by e itself.\n\n[343] Thus, if it exists, A is made true by itself. Thus truthmaking is not irreflexive, and\n\n[344] therefore neither is grounding.\n\n[345] But there is another reason why grounding is not irreflexive, since alethic-- fact grounding also fails irreflexivity. To see this, consider the truth--teller, B: <B\n\n[346] is true>. This proposition says of itself that it is true. Let us suppose that B is\n\n[347] indeed true. What grounds the fact that it is true? The fact stated by B itself. But\n\n[348] what B states is that it itself is true. Thus, if B is true, the fact that B is true\n\n[349] grounds the fact that B is true. Therefore, alethic--fact grounding is not irreflexive.\n\n[350] (4), (7), and (8). Tuomas Tahko (2013) argues that what he calls truth--grounding is not\n\n[351] transitive, and what he calls truth--grounding seems to be what I am calling here alethic--fact\n\n[352] grounding. Simplifying a little bit, he argues that while the fact that bottle b exists is grounded in\n\n[353] the fact that b has a stable macrophysical structure, and the fact that b has a stable macrophysical\n\n[354] structure is grounded in the fact that the Pauli Exclusion Principle holds, it is not the case that the\n\n[355] fact that b exists is grounded in the fact that the Pauli Exclusion principle holds (2013: 336-337).\n\n[356] The problem with this is that it does not seem to counterexemplify the transitivity of truth-- grounding in particular, since no fact involved in this case is a fact about something’s being true.\n\n[357] But I do not think the example even works against the transitivity of grounding in general. Tahko\n\n[358] thinks that general laws like the Pauli Exclusion Principle in no way contribute towards\n\n[359] answering the question about the existence of bottle b (2013: 337). But if the fact that the Pauli\n\n[360] Exclusion principle holds grounds the fact that b has a stable macrophysical structure, and this\n\n[361] latter fact grounds the fact that b exists, then I see no reason, other than assuming that\n\n[362] transitivity must fail, why the fact that the Pauli Exclusion Principle holds should not ground the\n\n[363] fact that bottle b exists or even the fact that <bottle b exists> is true. One might try reformulating\n\n[364] the example as follows: the fact that <b has a stable macrophysical structure> is true grounds the\n\n[365] fact that <b exists> is true, the fact that the Pauli Exclusion Principle holds grounds the fact that\n\n[366] <b has a stable macrophysical structure> is true, but it is not the case that the fact that the Pauli\n\n[367] Exclusion Principle holds grounds the fact that <b exists> is true. But this reformulation has the\n\n[368] additional problem that the first claim, that the fact that <b has a stable macrophysical structure>\n\n[369] is true grounds the fact that <b exists> is true, seems incorrect: what grounds the truth of <b\n\n[370] exists> is, in any case, something about b, say that it has a stable macrophysical structure, but not\n\n[371] something about the truth of another proposition.\n\n[372] 12\n\n[373] And since alethic--fact grounding is a species of grounding, grounding is not\n\n[374] irreflexive.8 (Note that if A is its own truthmaker, it is its only truthmaker, and if\n\n[375] the fact that B is true is grounded by the fact that B is true, the fact that B is true\n\n[376] is its sole ground. This means that A is its own full ground and the fact that B is\n\n[377] true is its own full ground).9\n\n[378] Kripke has proposed a theory of truth in which the concept of\n\n[379] groundedness plays a central role. The truthbearers in Kripke’s theory are\n\n[380] sentences. On Kripke’s theory, the truth--teller, the sentence that says of itself\n\n[381] that it is true, is ungrounded (Kripke: 2011: 85, 91). Thus what I am proposing\n\n[382] here seems to conflict with Kripke’s theory of truth.\n\n[383] But it is important to appreciate the real point of conflict. On Kripke’s\n\n[384] theory that a sentence is ungrounded basically means that its truth--value is not\n\n[385] ultimately determined by non--semantic facts (Kripke 2011: 88). The motivation\n\n[386] of Kripke’s theory is to give an explanation of truth that avoids the semantic\n\n[387] paradoxes, and he does this in a way that accounts for how someone who\n\n[388] initially does not understand the truth predicate, but who understands other\n\n[389] non--semantic predicates, would learn to use the truth predicate (2011: 85). At\n\n[390] the base of Kripke’s construction there are sentences featuring only non-- semantic predicates. The assignment of truth--values to sentences featuring non-- semantic predicates determines a minimal fixed point in which the truth value of\n\n[391] sentences featuring semantic predicates is ultimately determined by the truth\n\n[392] value of sentences featuring only non--semantic predicates. The sentences that do\n\n[393] not have a truth--value in the minimal fixed points are ungrounded. Thus for\n\n[394] Kripke ‘ungrounded sentence’ means ‘sentence not ultimately grounded in non-- semantic facts’.\n\n[395] But, as Kripke is of course aware, the truth--teller is not a paradoxical\n\n[396] sentence. The truth--teller can consistently be assigned the truth--value true, and\n\n\n===== Asymmetry examples =====\n[514] 6. If truthmaking and alethic--fact grounding are asymmetric, they must be\n\n[515] irreflexive. Their non--irreflexivity, however, guarantees their non--asymmetry\n\n[516] only by guaranteeing that cases of symmetric truthmaking and alethic--fact\n\n[517] grounding are cases of reflexive truthmaking and alethic--fact grounding. But if\n\n[518] what I have said above is accepted, it will not be difficult to see that truthmaking\n\n[519] and grounding are non--asymmetric in the full sense of there being distinct\n\n[520] entities that ground each other.\n\n[521] Consider the propositions C = <D exists> and D = <C exists>, and assume\n\n[522] that both of them exist. The truthmaker of C is D, and the truthmaker of D is C.\n\n[523] Therefore truthmaking is not asymmetric, and so neither is grounding.\n\n[524] Now consider the propositions E = <F is true> and F = <E is true>, and\n\n[525] assume they are both true. If so, given what they say, the fact that E is true\n\n[526] grounds the fact that F is true, and the fact that F is true grounds the fact that E is\n\n[527] true.10 Therefore, alethic--fact grounding is not asymmetric, and so neither is\n\n[528] grounding.\n\n[529] But there is a natural objection to this. This is that grounding must be\n\n[530] asymmetric because the ground is more fundamental than the grounded, and\n\n[531] being more fundamental than is an asymmetric relation (cf. Rosen 2010: 116,\n\n[532] Barnes 2014, Bennett forthcoming). The same kind of consideration can be used\n\n[533] to argue that grounding must be irreflexive, since being more fundamental than is\n\n[534] an irreflexive relation.\n\n[535] 10 Thompson (2014) has given a similar example.\n\n[536] 17\n\n[537] Now, the relations of grounding and being more fundamental than are not\n\n[538] the same relation. For something might be more fundamental than another thing\n\n[539] without grounding it. This will be the case, for instance, if the former thing is\n\n[540] fundamental and the latter one is grounded, but grounded in something other\n\n[541] than the former thing. But the point that grounding must be irreflexive and\n\n[542] asymmetric need not rely on an identification of the relations of grounding and\n\n[543] being more fundamental than but only on the fact that when x grounds y, x must\n\n[544] be more fundamental than y. But I shall argue that either the fact that x grounds y\n\n\n===== Fundamentality objection =====\n[545] does not entail that x is more fundamental than y or there is no reason to think\n\n[546] that it does.\n\n[547] Let us assume that the more fundamental than relation is asymmetric and\n\n[548] irreflexive. But what is it to be more fundamental than something else? One\n\n[549] answer is that it is to be closer to a fundamental (that is, ungrounded) entity than\n\n[550] another thing (where it is understood that a fundamental entity is as close to a\n\n[551] fundamental entity - itself - as anything can be). And the obvious way to\n\n[552] understand this idea is that x is closer to a fundamental entity than y if and only\n\n[553] if, in their chain of grounding, there are fewer elements between x and a\n\n[554] fundamental entity than between y and a fundamental entity.\n\n[555] Now the relation of grounding is not restricted to finite chains. Nothing in\n\n[556] the idea of grounding excludes the possibility of chains of grounding containing\n\n[557] infinitely many distinct elements and such that there is no first element, no\n\n[558] ungrounded ground. For instance, if wholes are grounded in their parts, a gunky\n\n[559] whole, i.e. a whole all of whose parts have proper parts, would be at the top of an\n\n[560] infinitely long chain of grounding with no first element. Now take two members\n\n[561] of such a chain, x and y, such that x grounds y. Is x more fundamental than y? No,\n\n[562] for there is no fundamental entity in their chain of grounding, and therefore it is\n\n[563] not the case that there are fewer elements between x and a fundamental entity\n\n[564] than between y and a fundamental entity.\n\n[565] If so, that x grounds y does not entail that x is more fundamental than y,\n\n[566] and so the fact that reflexive and symmetric cases of grounding are cases in\n\n[567] which the more fundamental than relation does not obtain between the ground\n\n[568] and the grounded is no objection to them. Reflexive and symmetric cases of\n\n[569] grounding are like bottomless chains of grounding: cases of grounding without\n\n[570] 18\n\n[571] different degrees of fundamentality. And in all three cases the reason why there\n\n[572] are no different degrees of fundamentality is that there are no fundamental\n\n[573] entities in the chains of grounding: neither bottomless chains of grounding, nor\n\n[574] ‘reflexive’ chains of grounding, nor ‘symmetric’ chains of grounding, contain\n\n[575] fundamental, ungrounded entities.11\n\n[576] It might be objected that there cannot be bottomless grounding chains,\n\n[577] that is, that every chain must end in an ungrounded ground. I do not see why\n\n[578] there cannot be such ‘bottomless’ chains. But I do not need their possibility to\n\n[579] make my point. For it is sufficient that there can be dense chains of grounding,\n\n[580] that is, chains of grounding such that between any two elements there is a\n\n[581] further element. In this case it will also be true that no element of such a chain\n\n[582] will be closer to a fundamental entity than any other element of it (except, of\n\n[583] course, when the member in question is the ungrounded ground of the chain).\n\n[584] It might be objected that the real number line is dense but one can make\n\n[585] sense of one positive real being closer to zero than another positive real. But this\n\n[586] is because we can independently make sense of the notion of one positive real\n\n[587] being bigger or smaller than another one. In this case we are working with a\n\n[588] notion of relative fundamentality according to which x is closer to a fundamental\n\n[589] entity than y if and only if, in their respective chains of grounding, there are\n\n[590] fewer elements between x and a fundamental entity than between y and a\n\n[591] fundamental entity.\n\n[592] A better objection would be against the characterization I have given of\n\n[593] what it is to be more fundamental than something else. Perhaps x is more\n\n[594] 11 Note that there is a sense in which a self--grounder is more fundamental than something\n\n\n===== More fundamentality continuation =====\n[595] grounded in something else, even if the self--grounder is not an ungrounded entity. This has two\n\n[596] consequences. One is that the idea of being more fundamental than, understood as being closer to\n\n[597] a fundamental, ungrounded, entity, cannot be straightforwardly extended to apply to cases of\n\n[598] entities belonging to different chains of grounding. For something that is its own sole ground is\n\n[599] no closer to an ungrounded entity than something that is grounded in an ungrounded entity, and\n\n[600] yet the former seems more fundamental than the latter. The other consequence is that a self-- grounder that grounds something else is more fundamental than what it grounds, even if it is not\n\n[601] closer to an ungrounded entity. Thanks to Paul Audi for making me think of these points. The\n\n[602] solution is to redefine, in the context of the present definition of being more fundamental than, a\n\n[603] fundamental entity as one that is either ungrounded or has no grounds other than itself. Even\n\n[604] under this definition of a fundamental entity it is still the case that no member of an infinitely\n\n[605] long descending chain of grounding will be closer to a fundamental entity than any other member\n\n[606] of it, and that the members of ‘reflexive’ and ‘symmetric’ chains of grounding (at least the kinds\n\n[607] of such chains I have considered in this paper) will not be closer to a fundamental entity than any\n\n[608] other member of them.\n\n[609] 19\n\n[610] fundamental than y if and only if every ground of x is a ground of y but not vice\n\n[611] versa? This definition is satisfied by suitable pairs of elements in grounding\n\n[612] chains with infinitely many elements, whether such chains have an ungrounded\n\n[613] first element or not.12 But, if this is what being more fundamental than is, what\n\n[614] reasons are there for thinking that if x grounds y, x must be more fundamental\n\n[615] than y? There do not seem to be any. Indeed, there seem to be no reasons why if\n\n[616] x grounds y then everything in virtue of which x is F is something in virtue of\n\n[617] which y is F, but not vice versa. Furthermore, my counterexamples to the\n\n[618] irreflexivity and the asymmetry of grounding are counterexamples to the idea\n\n[619] that if x grounds y, every ground of x must be a ground of y but not vice versa. So,\n\n[620] not only do there seem to be no reasons to think that if x grounds y, x must be\n\n[621] more fundamental than y in this sense, there are actually reasons against it.\n\n[622] Finally, one might say that x’s being more fundamental than y means that\n\n[623] x grounds y but is not grounded in it. But, again, there seem to be no reason for\n\n[624] thinking that if this is what being more fundamental than something means, then\n\n[625] grounding something entails being more fundamental than it. For there is no\n\n[626] reason to think that if x grounds y then, although y is F in virtue of x, x is not F in\n\n[627] virtue of y. And, again, my examples constitute reasons against the idea that if x\n\n[628] grounds y then x is not grounded in y.\n\n[629] Analogous replies could be made if someone objected that truthmaking\n\n[630] must be asymmetric because if x is a truthmaker of y, x must be more\n\n[631] fundamental than y.\n\n[632] So these objections can be repelled. If my point that grounding is not\n\n[633] asymmetric stands, it follows that grounding does not entail being more\n\n[634] 12 Three comments on the definition. (a) According to this definition a fundamental thing is more\n\n[635] fundamental than what it grounds since they vacuously satisfy the condition that every ground of\n\n[636] the fundamental thing is a ground of what it grounds and non--vacuously satisfy the condition\n\n[637] that not every ground of what the fundamental thing grounds is a ground of it. (b) As formulated,\n\n[638] this definition restricts the relation of being more fundamental than to pairs of things that are\n\n[639] elements of the same grounding chain. If something like this definition is correct, then whether it\n\n[640] can be extended to apply to pairs of things from different chains should be investigated. But this\n\n[641] is not the topic of this paper and the objection I am considering does not depend on whether or\n\n[642] not such a definition can be thus extended. (c) As formulated, this definition assumes the\n\n[643] transitivity of grounding. A more careful formulation of the definition, which does not assume the\n\n[644] transitivity of grounding, says that x is more fundamental than y if and only if the transitive\n\n[645] closure of the grounds of x is a subset of the transitive closure of the grounds of y, but not vice\n\n[646] versa. But it is only for simplicity that I gave the less careful formulation and, anyway, the point I\n\n[647] make in the text against the less careful version of the definition would also apply to the more\n\n[648] careful version.\n\n[649] 20\n\n[650] fundamental than. Thus it follows that grounding is not a relation of relative\n\n[651] fundamentality, to use an expression of Bennett’s (Bennett, forthcoming).\n\n[652] But there is another possible objection to my arguments that grounding is\n\n[653] not asymmetric. This is that grounding is a kind of explanation and explanations\n\n[654] are asymmetric. For instance, Fine says that ‘there is a plausible demand on\n\n[655] ground or explanation that we are unable to evade. For given a truth that stands\n\n[656] in need of explanation, one naturally supposes that it should have a “completely\n\n[657] satisfactory” explanation, one that does not involve cycles and terminates in\n\n[658] truths that do not stand in need of an explanation’ (Fine 2010: 105). This appeal\n\n[659] to the asymmetry of explanation to defend the asymmetry of grounding is not\n\n[660] uncommon (Audi 2012a: 102, Rosen 2010: 116).\n\n[661] Now, it is true that explanations, in the sense of propositions put forward\n\n[662] by an enquirer to advance our understanding, are asymmetric. Let us call\n\n[663] explanations in this sense epistemic explanations. But there is no reason to\n\n[664] extrapolate the formal features of epistemic explanations to grounding. I think\n\n[665] that there is a close connection between epistemic explanations and grounding,\n\n[666] namely that explanations track determinative relations and since grounding is a\n\n[667] determinative relation, many explanations track grounding relations (cf.\n\n[668] Rodriguez--Pereyra 2005: 28). But it does not follow that grounding must be\n\n[669] asymmetric because epistemic explanations are. Indeed, it does not even follow\n\n[670] that every relation of grounding must be susceptible of being tracked or\n\n[671] represented by an epistemic explanation.\n\n[672] But the thought might be that there is a non--epistemic asymmetric sort of\n\n[673] explanation and that grounding is such a non--epistemic sort of explanation or is\n\n[674] at least a species of it.13 Something like this is what Fine seems to be assuming in\n\n[675] the passage I quoted above, when he speaks of ‘grounding or explanation’. But,\n\n[676] whatever Fine was assuming, the idea that grounding is a non--epistemic\n\n[677] asymmetric sort of explanation cannot be used as an objection against my points\n\n\n===== Conclusion =====\n[721] 8. To conclude, truthmaking is neither transitive, nor irreflexive, nor asymmetric,\n\n[722] and alethic--fact grounding is neither irreflexive nor asymmetric. Since those two\n\n[723] relations are species or cases of grounding, grounding is neither transitive, nor\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
