": [\n      \"Skipped 1 unsupported external-link candidate(s).\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"schema_version\": \"source_record_v1\",\n  \"source_extraction\": {\n    \"converter_name\": \"pdftotext\",\n    \"converter_version\": null,\n    \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n    \"status\": \"success\",\n    \"warnings\": []\n  },\n  \"source_id\": \"source-02-grounding-is-not-a-strict-order--7c76f525b4e3\",\n  \"source_kind\": \"file\",\n  \"source_locator\": \"02-grounding-is-not-a-strict-order.pdf\",\n  \"source_media_type\": \"application/pdf\",\n  \"source_type\": \"file\",\n  \"title\": \"02-grounding-is-not-a-strict-order\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"reasoning","text":"**Analyzing grounding claims**\n\nI’m considering some claims about grounding. The first claim is that grounding isn’t a strict order, mainly because it doesn’t follow transitive, irreflexive, or asymmetric properties. Then, I’m thinking about truthmaking as a specific case of grounding, along with alethic-fact grounding also fitting within that category. Lastly, there’s a counterexample from Schaffer that supposedly demonstrates grounding's non-transitivity, but it seems unreliable since it depends on inconsistent counterfactual necessity.\n**Considering claim object parameters**\n\nI’m thinking about whether the claim object needs an additionalProperties parameter. It allows additional fields to be true, which can be useful. If I include a \"source_id\" per claim, the schema supports it, but I don't want to invent unnecessary fields. Claims don’t have required text, but they should include something. For evidence excerpts, they could be arrays or strings, and I need to make sure the excerpts aren’t too long."}}
